 namespace {

-// The regular pool is sized from the memory free when the process starts.
-//
-// On AROS a pool is physically backed (see kPoolMaxSize), and until exec has
-// an aligned allocator it costs twice its size: AllocPagesWithAlignOffset
-// over-reserves to align the pool to itself and cannot give the slack back
-// (JOB-0104 exec-allocmemaligned-proposal.md). A fixed 1 GiB regular pool is
-// therefore a fixed 2 GiB bite out of the machine for every process, which is
-// what capped a 12 GiB guest at browser + one child (JOB-0104 tset12): the
-// third process found a largest free block of 1.18 GiB and PA_CHECKed.
-//
-// So: the largest power of two at or below a quarter of free memory, which
-// keeps the pool's 2x reservation at or below half of what is free, and at or
-// below half of the largest free block, which is what the over-reserve needs
-// in one piece. The browser, first in, still gets kRegularPoolSize; each child
-// takes a share of what is left, so the guest degrades to smaller renderers
-// instead of to none. Below kMinRegularPoolSize a renderer cannot do useful
-// work anyway; the pool reservation then fails and the process dies in
-// HandlePoolAllocFailure, which is JOB-0095's sad tab once the browser can
-// see a child die.
-//
-// The result is cached: Init() and UninitForTesting() must see the same size,
-// and a second AvailMem() after the pools are reserved would see less.
+// Native mappings have contiguous virtual addresses and separately allocated
+// physical pages. Reservations are eagerly backed, so keep the temporary 2x
+// alignment reservation within half the free memory. Native trim releases the
+// alignment slack immediately. The largest physical block does not limit this
+// allocation. Cache the size so pool initialization and teardown agree.
 constexpr size_t kMinRegularPoolSize = kGiB / 8;
 constexpr size_t kFreeMemoryDivisor = 4;

 // Ceiling for a child that is neither a renderer nor the browser: the GPU
 // process and the utility processes (network, storage, audio, ...) never come
-// near the pool the free-memory rule would give them, and every byte of it is
-// physically backed twice. The browser and renderers keep the full rule.
+// near the pool the free-memory rule would give them. Their reservations are
+// eagerly backed too. The browser and renderers keep the full rule.
 //
 // The process type is read from the argument string CreateNewProc() gave this
 // process (NP_Arguments, launch_aros.cc), since the pool is reserved long
@@ -206,9 +189,7 @@

   const IPTR free_bytes = AvailMem(MEMF_PUBLIC);
   const IPTR largest = AvailMem(MEMF_PUBLIC | MEMF_LARGEST);
-  const size_t budget =
-      std::min(static_cast<size_t>(free_bytes) / kFreeMemoryDivisor,
-               static_cast<size_t>(largest) / 2);
+  const size_t budget = static_cast<size_t>(free_bytes) / kFreeMemoryDivisor;
   size_t size = std::bit_floor(budget);
   const size_t cap = ArosProcessTypePoolCap(max_size);
   size = std::clamp(size, kMinRegularPoolSize, cap);
