-// KrnAllocPages() currently provides physically backed pages rather than a
-// sparse virtual reservation. Keep the pre-MMU pools bounded until AROS can
-// reserve and commit address space independently. The regular pool also
-// carries malloc() when the allocator shim is in use, so it gets the maximum;
-// the smaller pools size themselves in partition_address_space.h.
-//
-// The pool must be aligned to its own size, and PartitionAlloc gets that by
-// reserving twice the pool and trimming - so the machine has to hand over one
-// contiguous block of 2 * kPoolMaxSize, physically. At 2 GiB that is a 4 GiB
-// block, which an installed system can find but a Live CD cannot: booting the
-// ISO on a 12 GB machine leaves a largest free block of 3.57 GB and Chromium
-// dies in PostEarlyInitialization before it ever draws a window. 1 GiB keeps
-// the reservation at 2 GiB and fits.
+// Native mappings are virtually contiguous but eagerly physically backed.
+// Bound pool capacity until reservation and physical commit are independent.
+// Alignment temporarily reserves twice the pool; native trim releases slack.
+// The regular pool also backs malloc() when the allocator shim is enabled.
 constexpr size_t kPoolMaxSize = 1 * kGiB;
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID) || BUILDFLAG(IS_IOS) || \
     BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_POINTER_COMPRESSION)
